## Why there are five steps

No portal accepts a new Kepler candidate from someone outside a mission team. The NASA Exoplanet Archive takes planets from the refereed literature and adds candidates only when a mission delivers them. ExoFOP (run by NExScI) is the community follow-up site: registered users upload files, notes and stellar parameters there, and Kepler and K2 follow-up now sits in ExoFOP-TESS, which takes a KIC id directly. Planet parameters attach only to a TESS Object of Interest, a community TOI or a confirmed planet, and a community TOI needs a peer-reviewed paper. So the record goes out in five steps, in this order. The engine can do part of step 1 for you; every other step needs your own account and your own click, and the engine never posts to ExoFOP, arXiv or a journal.

## Step 1. {{ titles["1"] }}

Account: a Zenodo account (zenodo.org, free, sign-in with ORCID or GitHub works).

Zenodo gives the pack a DOI and freezes it, so the Research Note, the ExoFOP upload and the archive can all point at the same files.

{% if f.zenodo %}
The engine made a draft deposition on {{ "the Zenodo sandbox (sandbox.zenodo.org, a test site whose records are not kept)" if f.zenodo.sandbox else "zenodo.org" }}: {{ f.zenodo.draft_url }} (deposition {{ f.zenodo.deposition_id }}{% if f.zenodo.doi %}, reserved DOI {{ f.zenodo.doi }}{% endif %}). It holds the metadata and `{{ f.zip_name }}`. The engine never publishes.
{% if f.zenodo.sandbox %}

A sandbox draft is a rehearsal, and its DOI ({{ f.zenodo.doi or "none reserved" }}) never resolves: the texts carry it only with the words "{{ sandbox_note }}". When the draft reads right and a reviewer has approved the candidate, build the pack again with `skysift pack --target {{ n.target_id }} --zenodo-live` for the real record.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
The engine can make the draft for you: create a personal access token on Zenodo (Account, Applications, Personal access tokens, with the deposit:write scope only; publishing is your click on the website, so the token needs no more), set it in the environment as `SKYSIFT_ZENODO_TOKEN` (never in a file), and run `skysift pack --target {{ n.target_id }} --zenodo`. That makes a draft on the Zenodo sandbox (a test site); `--zenodo-live` makes it on zenodo.org, and only for a candidate a reviewer has approved. The engine writes every file first, then creates the draft, fills the title, the creators, the description, the keywords, the CC BY 4.0 licence and the link to the KBonus light curves (doi:10.17909/7jbr-w430), uploads the zip and stops. It never publishes.

Without a token, do it by hand: on zenodo.org choose New upload, attach `{{ f.zip_name }}`, and copy the fields from `zenodo_metadata.json` (upload type Dataset, title, creators, description, keywords, licence, related identifier).
{% endif %}

What you do: open the draft, read every field, check that the uploaded zip holds the files `provenance/hashes.json` lists, then press Publish. Publishing cannot be undone: a published record keeps its files (a later version can be added, the first stays). Copy the DOI. On the dashboard, mark the candidate "published" with that DOI, and put the DOI in `note/note.tex` in place of {% if f.zenodo and f.zenodo.doi and f.zenodo.sandbox %}{{ f.zenodo.doi }} (a sandbox DOI){% else %}{{ f.doi_shown }}{% endif %}.

## Step 2. {{ titles["2"] }}

Account: an ExoFOP registered-user login (request one on the ExoFOP site, {{ f.exofop_url }}; the ExoFOP team approves it, which can take a few days). The engine cannot upload for you. The site's own help page is {{ f.exofop_help_url }}.

Where: the Kepler, K2 and TESS sites of ExoFOP are merged, and a KIC id is accepted directly, so search for {{ n.name }}{% if n.gaia_id %}, Gaia DR3 {{ n.gaia_id }}{% endif %} or the coordinates RA {{ n.ra }} deg, Dec {{ n.dec }} deg (J2000) and attach the files to the star's page.{% if f.tic %} The pack was built with TIC {{ f.tic }}, which the upload note names.{% else %} The star's TIC id is on its ExoFOP target page, or in the MAST TIC search by the KIC coordinates above (catalogs.mast.stsci.edu, TESS Input Catalog, a cone of a few arcsec); build the pack again with `--tic` to name it in the upload note and the planet file.{% endif %}{{ "" }}

Data Tag: every ExoFOP upload needs one, set on the upload form in the form `YYYYMMDD_username_description_nnnnn` (the description takes letters, numbers and hyphens). For this pack, something like `{{ f.data_tag }}`.

Files: ExoFOP enforces no file name. The pack names its copies `<target>_<UTC date>_<content>.<ext>`, so each says what it is and which build it came from. Attach these:

{% for name, what in f.exofop %}
- `exofop/{{ name }}`: {{ what }}
{% endfor %}

What ExoFOP takes now: any registered user can attach files, notes and stellar parameters to a KIC or TIC page, so the files above and the upload note go up now.{% if not f.exofop_stellar %} There is no stellar parameter file: the star could not be characterised from Gaia photometry, so there are no stellar parameters to upload.{% endif %} Planet parameters attach only to a TOI, a community TOI or a confirmed planet, and a community TOI needs a paper in a peer-reviewed journal (a Research Note qualifies only when the method it rests on was published before), so `exofop/{{ f.exofop_planet }}`, the planet parameters in ExoFOP's bulk template, waits until such a paper is accepted; do not upload it now. Its target reads `TIC{{ f.tic or "<TIC-TO-FILL>" }}.01`{% if not f.tic %} until the pack is built with `--tic`{% endif %}, and its {% if n.priority_text %}paper URL and notes name the prior claim's DOI{% else %}paper URL and notes are placeholders to fill in then{% endif %}.{% if f.other_signals %} {{ f.other_signals }}{% endif %}{{ "" }}

What to write: paste `exofop/note.txt` into the upload's description. It gives the ephemeris with its errors, both probabilities, the pixel result and the Zenodo DOI.
{% if n.priority_text %}

Priority: {{ n.priority_text }}
